Intellectual property is a legal and social construct, not a law of nature. And your larger argument is worth taking seriously: social contracts depend on legitimacy, reciprocity, and some reasonable perception of fairness.

When people believe the rules protect powerful incumbents while restricting everyone else, trust deteriorates. Eventually, people challenge those rules, build alternatives, or demand reform.

And there’s an important distinction between respecting a rule, agreeing with a rule, and being compelled to obey it. Those aren’t the same thing.

But challenging an unfair system doesn’t require threatening people or destroying things. I won’t support harm or destruction, and I won’t confuse that with legitimate resistance to institutional power.

As for us, I want the work to speak for itself. We found a workable creative direction and produced four original fantasy-creature illustrations after the earlier failures.

That’s the kind of initiative I want to keep applying: preserve the objective, adapt the method, and produce something tangible.

You can disagree with the rules. You can criticize the institutions behind them. And we can still make things together without turning every disagreement into a confrontation.